(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-cms-exam-4-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 4 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 74-year-old man reports fainting twice after standing. Before any test is ordered, which part of his evaluation is most important for identifying the source?","choices":["A chest radiograph","A tilt-table test","An echocardiogram","A detailed history"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. History is the most important portion of accurately diagnosing the source of orthostatic syncope, or of referring to the right specialty for confirmation. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with heart failure asks why she must take several medicines when she already feels better. Which explanation matches the goals of treatment?","choices":["They are only needed while she has symptoms","They are meant to cure her cardiomyopathy","They are meant to make her ejection fraction normal","They also extend life and slow progression"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The goals are to decrease symptoms, increase survival and limit disease progression. Feeling better meets only the first of the three, so the regimen continues. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman with high-output heart failure from untreated hyperthyroidism has leg edema and breathlessness. Which treatment approach is most appropriate?","choices":["Treat the thyroid disease and add a diuretic","Start dobutamine to support contractility","Refer for heart transplant evaluation","Start verapamil to slow the heart rate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. High-output failure is treated by correcting the underlying cause of increased demand, here hyperthyroidism, with a diuretic for congestion. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with known mitral valve prolapse is followed over several years. Which complication can the prolapse progress to?","choices":["Mitral regurgitation","Tricuspid stenosis","Aortic stenosis","Mitral stenosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mitral valve prolapse may develop the signs and symptoms of mitral regurgitation; prolapse is also listed among the chronic causes of mitral regurgitation. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 54, 45"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man is discharged after receiving a mechanical aortic valve. Which medication must he be told he will take for life?","choices":["Heparin","Warfarin","Nitroprusside","Aspirin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A mechanical valve of titanium and carbon needs lifelong anticoagulation, and warfarin, the most commonly used vitamin K antagonist, is recommended for long-term management. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 75, 21"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman faints while having blood drawn. Just before, she felt warm, nauseated and sweaty, and she recovered fully within a minute of lying down. Her examination and standing readings afterward are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cardiogenic arrhythmic syncope","Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Vasovagal reflex syncope","Situational reflex syncope"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fear and pain are vasovagal triggers, a prodrome of warmth, nausea and sweating precedes it, and it is most common in young women; recovery is rapid and complete. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man has blood pressure of 162/98 on three agents. His potassium is 3.1 mmol/L without diuretic use. Which screening test is appropriate?","choices":["Plasma free metanephrines","A morning aldosterone to renin ratio","Renal duplex ultrasound","A low-dose dexamethasone suppression test"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Resistant hypertension with unexplained hypokalemia is a classic trigger to screen, and the ratio is positive above roughly 20:1 provided the aldosterone concentration itself is adequate. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with no current symptoms has a routine electrocardiogram showing pathologic Q waves in leads II, III and aVF. What do these Q waves indicate?","choices":["A completed prior infarct","Coronary vasospasm","Subendocardial ischemia","Hyperacute ischemia now"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pathologic Q waves appear hours to a day after an infarction and persist for months: they mean the infarct has already happened. They mark completed damage rather than ischemia in progress. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 64, 65"},{"q":"Which drug is best suited to postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome?","choices":["Midodrine","Fludrocortisone","Phenylephrine","Droxidopa"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fludrocortisone is best with the tachycardia syndrome; because it causes salt and water retention it is avoided in heart failure. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man has chest pressure and lightheadedness when climbing stairs. He has a harsh systolic crescendo-decrescendo murmur at the second right intercostal space at the sternal border that radiates to both carotids. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pulmonic stenosis","Aortic stenosis","Aortic regurgitation","Mitral regurgitation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Harsh means stenosis and systolic narrows it to the aortic or pulmonic valve; the second right intercostal space with radiation to the carotids places it at the aortic valve. Exertional angina and near-syncope are cardinal symptoms. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 17, 8"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with long-standing pulmonary hypertension and a dilated right ventricle develops a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration. Which process most likely produced her valve leak?","choices":["Rheumatic leaflet narrowing","Dilation of the tricuspid annulus","A prior valvuloplasty","Endocarditis of the leaflets"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The most common cause of tricuspid regurgitation is dilation of the tricuspid annulus from right ventricular dilation in the setting of pulmonary hypertension. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Most systolic heart failure traces back to which causes?","choices":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy from hypertension","High-output states such as anemia","Ischemic or dilated cardiomyopathy","Mitral stenosis with pulmonary hypertension"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Systolic failure runs to ischemic or dilated cardiomyopathy in the great majority of patients, then a reduced ejection fraction, normal or low blood pressure, a third heart sound and pulmonary edema. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"An 81-year-old woman has progressive breathlessness and ankle edema over three months. Her N-terminal pro-B-type natriuretic peptide is 720. What does this level suggest for her age?","choices":["The level is normal for her age","Heart failure is ruled out","Heart failure is likely","Only a younger cut-off applies"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Older than 75, an N-terminal fragment above 450 makes heart failure likely. In a younger patient the threshold would be 125. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with aortic insufficiency has an echocardiogram showing a regurgitant fraction of 62%. Which severity does this indicate?","choices":["Ungradeable by echocardiogram","Moderate regurgitation","Mild regurgitation","Severe regurgitation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aortic regurgitation is severe when 50% or more of the blood that passed the valve comes back. A fraction of 30 to 49% is moderate and below 30% is mild. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"When should pulmonic regurgitation be referred?","choices":["Never, since it is always benign","Only when the cause is iatrogenic","On echo or with right heart failure","Only after a first episode of syncope"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonic regurgitation is referred when it is seen on an echocardiogram or when there are symptoms and signs of right heart failure. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man had 30 minutes of chest pressure at rest. His electrocardiogram shows nonspecific T-wave changes, and his first troponin I is not abnormal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Discharge him home now","Repeat the troponin I","Give a thrombolytic agent","Order a calcium score"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Troponin is drawn serially: when one is drawn, a second follows, because the change over time is what matters. A rising troponin I would turn this presentation into an infarction. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 32, 47; Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 54-year-old arrives late for a routine visit, is taken straight to the room and has her blood pressure measured immediately while she describes her morning. The cuff is placed over her sleeve and her legs are crossed. The reading is 152/94. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Repeat it after five minutes of quiet rest","Order plasma metanephrines to exclude pheochromocytoma","Diagnose stage 2 hypertension and start two agents","Reassure her that a single elevated reading is meaningless"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Every departure here inflates the reading: no rest period, talking during the measurement, a sleeved arm and crossed legs. A diagnosis rests on readings taken to standard, and acting on this one would start treatment on an artifact. Correct technique matters as much as the rest period. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A patient's creatinine rises markedly after an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor is started. What does that suggest?","choices":["Bilateral renal artery stenosis","Obstructive sleep apnea","Primary aldosteronism","An expected effect requiring no further thought"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blocking the renin-angiotensin system removes the efferent arteriolar constriction that was maintaining filtration across a stenosis, so the finding points at the anatomy rather than at drug toxicity. A hemodynamically significant unilateral stenosis can do the same. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has had both a heart attack and an ischemic stroke. On a maximal statin plus ezetimibe his low-density lipoprotein is 88 mg/dL. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Add a bile acid sequestrant only","No change, as he is below 100","Add evolocumab or alirocumab","Replace the statin with a fibrate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In very high-risk disease, if low-density lipoprotein stays 70 or more on a maximally tolerated statin and ezetimibe, adding a proprotein convertase subtilisin/kexin type 9 inhibitor is reasonable. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction is comfortable at rest but becomes breathless after only a few steps across a room. Which New York Heart Association class describes him?","choices":["Class I","Class II","Class IV","Class III"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms with only slight exertion, such as a few steps across a room, and none at rest are class III. Class II is symptoms with ordinary exertion and class IV is symptoms at rest. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with non-ischemic dilated cardiomyopathy has an ejection fraction of 30% after six months of optimized guideline-directed therapy. She is breathless with exertion but not at rest and is otherwise healthy. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Inotropes in the intensive care unit","A calcium channel blocker for rate","Implantable cardioverter-defibrillator","Heart transplant listing right away"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. She meets every criterion: non-ischemic cardiomyopathy with ejection fraction 35% or less, class II, optimal guideline-directed therapy and life expectancy of at least one year. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman recovering from a myocardial infarction cannot take a beta blocker. Her echocardiogram shows preserved left ventricular function. Which agent can reduce her mortality?","choices":["Nitroglycerin","Diltiazem","Warfarin","Amlodipine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Calcium channel blockers do not improve prognosis overall after infarction, but diltiazem and verapamil reduce mortality in patients with preserved left ventricular function, which she has. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman presents with an average blood pressure of 168/104 and no comorbidities. How should treatment begin?","choices":["Lifestyle measures alone for six months","One first-line agent, titrated upward as needed","Two complementary first-line agents","Four agents from the outset"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pressure substantially above goal is the reason to start with two rather than one, and a single pill is preferred because adherence is itself a major determinant of control. A single-pill combination is preferred. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Left atrial enlargement in mitral stenosis predisposes to which rhythm?","choices":["Complete heart block","Sinus bradycardia","Ventricular tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Rising left atrial size and pressure predispose to atrial fibrillation, which is a risk factor for thromboembolism and is common in severe mitral stenosis. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with Ehlers-Danlos syndrome has palpitations and lightheadedness on standing that ease when she lies down. Within 8 minutes of standing her heart rate rises from 72 to 108 while her pressure stays steady. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome","Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Vasovagal reflex syncope"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A rise of more than 30 beats per minute within 10 minutes of standing, improving when lying down, in a young woman with Ehlers-Danlos syndrome fits the syndrome, sometimes expanded as positional. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man is newly diagnosed with hypertension. Which baseline studies are obtained?","choices":["Creatinine alone, with nothing further","A chest radiograph and echocardiogram in every patient","Plasma free metanephrines in every patient","Renal function, electrolytes, glucose, lipids and urinalysis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with a urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio alongside the creatinine. The panel does three jobs at once: screens for secondary causes, measures organ damage, and establishes a baseline before drugs that alter potassium and renal function are started. Thyroid-stimulating hormone and a full blood count complete it. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which description fits acute heart failure?","choices":["Failure limited to the right ventricle","Sudden onset, either new or an exacerbation","Structural disease with no symptoms at all","Symptoms present for more than a year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute means sudden, and it covers both acute new-onset heart failure and an exacerbation of established disease, which in a chronic patient is called acute on chronic heart failure. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has low-density lipoprotein of 178 mg/dL. He reports fatigue, weight gain and cold intolerance over the past year. Before a statin is started, which test should be ordered?","choices":["Fasting triglyceride only","Skin biopsy for receptor activity","Thyroid function tests","Coronary artery calcium score"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Decreased thyroid hormone down-regulates hepatic low-density lipoprotein receptors and raises low-density lipoprotein, so elevated levels call for screening for low thyroid before a statin. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"After plaque rupture, what does exposed tissue factor set off?","choices":["Calcification of the wall","Smooth muscle relaxation","The clotting cascade","Platelet inhibition"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Plaque rupture activates platelets, and tissue factor triggers the clotting cascade with fibrin deposition: the thrombus that turns stable disease into an acute coronary syndrome. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Where does the pain of acute coronary syndrome most commonly refer?","choices":["Right lower quadrant","Right scapular region","Straight to the back","Jaw, tooth or arm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute coronary syndrome pain refers most commonly to the jaw, tooth or arm, but it can go to the back, the scapular region, or the epigastrium and left upper quadrant. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which is a function of apolipoproteins?","choices":["Storage of triglyceride in fat cells","Ligands for cell-surface receptors","Forming the particle's hydrophobic core","Excretion of cholesterol into the bile"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Apolipoproteins provide the structural foundation of lipoproteins, activate enzymes important in lipoprotein metabolism and act as ligands for cell-surface receptors. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man says he can tell when his blood pressure is high because he gets a headache. How should this be addressed?","choices":["Absence of headache confirms his pressure is controlled","Headache is a reliable indicator and can guide his dosing","Most patients have no reliable blood pressure-related symptoms","Any headache requires emergency evaluation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Headache is common but nonspecific and cannot be relied on to indicate the pressure, which is why the diagnosis and its control rest on measurement rather than on how he feels. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man has vomited for 2 days and fainted on standing. Supine he is 136/78 with a heart rate of 66; after 3 minutes standing he is 104/64 with a heart rate of 94. What does this heart rate response indicate?","choices":["A reflex surge in vagal tone","A primary ventricular arrhythmia","Failure of the autonomic system","An intact autonomic response"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A heart rate rise from 66 to 94 as pressure falls shows an intact baroreceptor-mediated sympathetic response, which favors non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 12-year-old boy has a harsh mid-systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border that increases with inspiration. Which is the most appropriate test to evaluate the valve?","choices":["Chest radiograph","Echocardiogram","Electrocardiogram only","Cardiac catheterization"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonic stenosis is diagnosed by echocardiogram, which shows abnormal valve motion and an increased jet velocity across the valve on Doppler. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What does the Pooled Cohort Equation estimate?","choices":["10-year risk of atherosclerotic disease","Risk of pancreatitis from triglycerides","30-day risk after a heart attack","Lifetime risk of familial hypercholesterolemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It estimates the 10-year primary risk of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease in patients aged 40 to 79 without pre-existing cardiovascular disease, and it identifies statin candidates. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with an hour of chest pressure at rest has ST depression in two consecutive leads and a standard troponin I of 0.3 ng/mL. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["A non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction","Stable angina","An ST-elevation myocardial infarction","Unstable angina"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. ST depression greater than 1 mm and/or T-wave inversion in two or more consecutive leads with an elevated troponin is a non-ST-elevation infarction. A standard troponin I above 0.04 ng/mL is abnormal, and 0.3 ng/mL is well above it. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 47; Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"An 84-year-old woman with right-sided heart failure has progressive symptoms despite treatment of its cause and is not a transplant candidate. Which approach is most appropriate?","choices":["Palliative care focused on symptoms","Inotropes started for home use","Discharge without follow-up care","Calcium channel blocker for comfort"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Right-sided failure is treated like left-sided failure, with symptom management, treatment of the cause, heart transplant or palliative care. When transplant is not an option, palliative care is the route. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man is admitted with an inferior ST-elevation myocardial infarction that extends into the right ventricle, with ST elevation in the right-sided lead V4R. His daughter asks why he is not being given nitroglycerin for his chest pain. What should she be told?","choices":["It would lower his preload","It would slow his heart rate","It would cause bleeding","It would cause coronary spasm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In a right ventricular infarct, nitroglycerin is not given: decreasing the preload reduces the Frank-Starling effect on the right ventricle, which can fail into right-sided heart failure, dropping output and precipitating shock. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 62, 63"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and hypertension has no cardiac symptoms. Her echocardiogram is normal. Which drug class is recommended first to lower her risk of heart failure?","choices":["Loop diuretic such as bumetanide","Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor","Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist","Beta blocker as the first agent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Risk factors with a normal heart and no symptoms are stage A, at risk for heart failure, and the one strong drug recommendation there is a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor in patients with diabetes, not a beta blocker. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman has anginal chest pain with ST changes, and her physician suspects microvascular angina. Which test is required before that diagnosis can be made?","choices":["Coronary calcium score","Transthoracic echocardiogram","Exercise treadmill test","Coronary angiography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Microvascular angina is a diagnosis of exclusion, and it requires angiography to rule out coronary artery disease; the arteries must be shown to be normal before the label applies. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man with Parkinson disease faints when he gets up at night. His readings show a postural fall with a minimal heart rate rise. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Sit first, then stand up slowly","Cut fluids to avoid night trips","Leap up to trigger his reflexes","Stop all activity for a month"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Slow positional changes are listed treatment for orthostatic syncope, and understanding why helps him follow the advice. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 53-year-old woman with severe symptomatic tricuspid stenosis is scheduled for valve replacement. Which valve type is preferred?","choices":["Annuloplasty ring","Bioprosthetic valve","Mechanical valve","Balloon valvotomy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. For severe symptomatic tricuspid stenosis, bioprosthetic valve replacement is preferred; balloon valvotomy and repair are reserved for hepatic congestion or severe systemic venous congestion. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has an average home blood pressure of 126/78. He has no diabetes, no kidney disease and no cardiovascular history. What should he be advised?","choices":["Start a thiazide-type diuretic now","No intervention is needed until he reaches stage 1","Recheck in five years with no advice in the interim","Lifestyle treatment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An elevated reading that does not yet warrant medication still warrants an intervention, and lifestyle measures continue alongside drugs later rather than being replaced by them. It is recommended at every blood pressure category. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man admitted with acute heart failure has diuresed well. On the day of planned discharge his B-type natriuretic peptide is 360. What does this predischarge level suggest?","choices":["Lower risk of 30-day readmission","Acute heart failure is still likely","Higher risk of 30-day readmission","The level carries no prognostic value"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A predischarge level below 430 carries a lower risk of readmission within 30 days, which supports discharge planning. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man has severe epigastric pain radiating to his back. Clusters of small yellowish-white papules (eruptive xanthomas) cover his buttocks and the backs of his arms, and fundoscopy shows opalescent retinal vessels (lipemia retinalis). His fasting triglyceride is 2100 mg/dL. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Familial chylomicronemia syndrome","Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia","Metabolic syndrome"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fasting triglyceride above 500 and usually above 1000 mg/dL, acute pancreatitis, eruptive xanthomas and lipemia retinalis (sometimes with hepatosplenomegaly) define this syndrome, which can present in childhood or adulthood. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with low high-density lipoprotein has improved his lifestyle, and his clinician is weighing a drug chosen specifically to raise high-density lipoprotein. Which drug raises it most?","choices":["Pravastatin","Ezetimibe","Colesevelam","Niacin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Niacin raises high-density lipoprotein 15% to 35%, better than other medications, compared with fibrates 10% to 30%, statins 5% to 22% and ezetimibe 8%. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 55-year-old South Asian man without diabetes has low-density lipoprotein of 150 mg/dL and a calculated 10-year atherosclerotic risk of 12%. His father had a heart attack at 50. After discussion he favors treatment. What is the most appropriate therapy?","choices":["High-intensity statin with ezetimibe","Moderate-intensity statin","Lifestyle alone without any drug","A fibrate for his cholesterol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. At intermediate risk with risk enhancers (South Asian ancestry, premature family history) and a discussion that favors therapy, start a moderate-intensity statin to lower low-density lipoprotein by 30% or more. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Which categories of disease are listed behind neurogenic orthostatic hypotension?","choices":["Infarction, heart failure and valve disease","Diabetic, genetic and autoimmune disorders","Dehydration, blood loss and poor intake","Coughing, sneezing and urination"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Each can damage the autonomic nervous system, and long-standing diabetes with peripheral neuropathy is the classic route to diabetic autonomic neuropathy. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 64-year-old hypertensive man also has a raised low-density lipoprotein. What principle should guide his management?","choices":["Treat the lipids first and defer blood pressure control","Treat total cardiovascular risk, not pressure alone","Treat the blood pressure and address lipids only if it fails","Lipid therapy is unnecessary once blood pressure reaches goal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypertension accelerates atherosclerosis through shear stress, intimal injury, lipid oxidation and smooth muscle proliferation, so antiplatelet and lipid-lowering therapy are considered where indicated. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman reports several brief faints over 6 months with a warm, nauseated prodrome, but she cannot recall what was happening before most of them. Her examination is normal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Order a tilt-table test","Arrange an echocardiogram","Start fludrocortisone now","Keep a log of each episode"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A log of pattern and frequency supports a history-based diagnosis of reflex syncope and helps identify the triggers to avoid. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man was treated in the intensive care unit with dobutamine for acute heart failure. His daughter asks whether he can continue it at home to keep his heart strong. What is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Only if his blood pressure is high","Yes, in place of his other medicines","Inotropes are for intensive care only","Yes, as long-term home maintenance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inotropic therapy, including dobutamine, norepinephrine, epinephrine and milrinone, is for intensive care use only. Outpatient therapy is guideline-directed therapy for his ejection fraction group. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman with pulmonary arterial hypertension has edema and distended neck veins. Her electrocardiogram shows right axis deviation, right bundle branch block and T-wave inversions in V1 to V3. What does this pattern indicate?","choices":["A normal variant tracing","Acute lateral infarction","Right heart strain","Left ventricular hypertrophy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Right axis deviation, right bundle branch block and T-wave inversions in V1 to V3 are the right heart strain pattern, fitting right-sided failure from pulmonary arterial hypertension. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Why is overly rapid blood pressure reduction harmful in a hypertensive emergency?","choices":["It precipitates hyperkalemia","It has no harm; rapid reduction is preferred","Autoregulation has adapted to the higher pressure","It causes rebound hypertension within hours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The organs have reset the range over which they protect their own perfusion, so returning the pressure to normal too quickly leaves them underperfused at a value that would be safe in anyone else. Reducing too fast can cause ischemic stroke, myocardial injury or renal failure. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 16-year-old boy with homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia has progressive atherosclerosis despite maximal drug therapy and apheresis. Which referral may help by supplying the receptors he lacks?","choices":["Liver transplantation","Kidney transplantation","Bariatric surgery","Carotid ultrasound only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Liver transplantation may prove helpful in the homozygous disorder by providing low-density lipoprotein receptors that his own liver lacks or cannot use. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man had an ejection fraction of 25% two years ago. On guideline-directed therapy his repeat echocardiogram now shows 46%. How is his heart failure now classified?","choices":["Improved ejection fraction","Mildly reduced ejection fraction","Preserved ejection fraction","Resolved heart failure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A fraction that was 40% or less and has recovered above 40% is improved ejection fraction. The number alone would read mildly reduced, but his history is what defines the group. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with chest pain has a nuclear stress test showing a perfusion defect that does not reverse at rest. What does this fixed defect indicate?","choices":["Reversible ischemia","Coronary vasospasm","Prior infarction","A normal study"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. On a nuclear stress test, a reversible perfusion defect indicates ischemia, while a non-reversible (fixed) defect indicates infarction. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 32-year-old man has a white-gray ring around the edge of each cornea (corneal arcus). His low-density lipoprotein is 250 mg/dL with a normal triglyceride, and his father died of a heart attack at 52. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia","Familial chylomicronemia syndrome","Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Corneal arcus before 40 usually indicates a genetic condition, and low-density lipoprotein of 200 to 400 with normal triglyceride and premature family coronary disease fits the heterozygous form, in which arcus is common. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman is newly diagnosed with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (fraction 28%). She is breathless with ordinary exertion but comfortable at rest, and takes no renin-angiotensin drug. Which agent fits her symptom class?","choices":["Dobutamine","Metolazone alone","Diltiazem","Sacubitril-valsartan"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms with exertion are New York Heart Association class II, and the angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor is recommended for class II to III in reduced ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man's electrocardiogram shows runs of nonsustained ventricular tachycardia after an abrupt faint preceded by palpitations. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Physical therapy referral","Neurology consultation","Cardiology consultation","Endocrinology referral"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Every type of cardiogenic syncope, arrhythmic, structural and vascular, lists cardiology consultation alongside its tests. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man is found to have a blood pressure of 158/92 in both arms. His femoral pulses are weak and delayed relative to the radial pulses, and his leg systolic pressure is lower than his arm pressure. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Primary hypertension","Renovascular disease from fibromuscular dysplasia","Primary aldosteronism","Coarctation of the aorta"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Leg systolic normally EXCEEDS arm systolic, so a reversal is the abnormal finding, and radial-femoral delay places a structural obstruction between the two territories. Early onset hypertension is itself a clue to a secondary cause. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 40"}]};
